ut when multiple tests are required, there's substantial block advantage—particularly for cases needing 2+ tests where the advantage jumps to 31-40 percentage points.\n\nNow examining the correlation structure and repo-level results. The block correlation is significant with ICC of 0.19, indicating tests aren't independent—we're seeing nearly 3x more all-correct instances than expected under independence. At the repository level, the Qwen3-4B model achieves 83.63% accuracy, substantially outperforming the 72.96% majority baseline, though there's a notable 6.44 percentage point gap between validation and test performance.\n\nComparing across model variants and approaches, the Qwen3-8B CLS reaches 85.60% with different seed configurations, while the CWM approach struggles with high unparseable rates across Qwen versions. DeepSeek's CLS variants show lower performance in the 71-75% range, and its CWM approach is severely hampered by parsing failures. The trajectory-based approach for DeepSeek achieves around 75% with reasonable stability across seeds. Zero-shot baselines for both models hover near 50%, and the re-repair analysis shows modest improvements from diagnostic information, with oracle performance reaching 44%.\n\nThe trace-then-judge strategy didn't work out—the model couldn't extract meaningful execution traces. However, the CWM generation itself performs well with 87.51% accuracy and perfect parseability, so the issue lies elsewhere in the pipeline. Now I'm thinking about how to structure the main results table, focusing on accuracy as the primary metric since not all entries have F1 scores available.\n\nGenerating markdown table... Generating markdown table... Now I'm looking at the complete block correlation analysis—the intraclass correlation coefficient is 0.1909 with a p-value below 0.001, and the design effect of 3.97 means we're observing about 2.81 times more all-correct instances than we'd expect under independence. I have all the data I need to write this up. I'll structure the paper carefully, avoiding em-dashes by using parentheses instead, and I'll rephrase any -ing constructions to use active voice. Let me start drafting the full paper and compile the bibliography with the key references on process supervision, outcome supervision comparisons, and the relevant code-based approaches. WE-bench (Jimenez et al. 2024), CRUXEval (Gu et al. 2024), and SWE-RM or similar trajectory verifier are reasonable references to include. Now I'm mapping out where each experiment from the JSON appears in the paper—I need to make sure every single one has a corresponding section or table. Let me work through this systematically: the CWM generation baseline and multi-seed comparisons go into Table 1 and Section 4.1, the trace-then-judge ablation is in 4.7, trajectory-level finetuning results are in Table 1 and 4.1, model scaling experiments with the 8B variants appear in Table 1 and 4.2, and I'm continuing to map the remaining experiments across the tables and sections. I've finished mapping out all the experiments to their paper sections—25 of the 27 are actual experiments, with 2 being meta-analyses. Now I'm reconsidering the section structure to better organize the narrative, moving from foundational concepts through scaling studies and diagnostics, then into repository-level analysis and ablations before wrapping up with discussion.\n\nFor the worst-tier results, I need to be careful about positioning—emphasizing the methodological contributions like TOST and block-correlation analysis as the core novelty, treating the repository-level findings as exploratory rather than definitive, and being honest about what the data doesn't show. I'm also consolidating experiments that are just different seeds or epochs of the same setup to avoid redundant tables, so I can present the results more cleanly.\n\nNow I'm working through the table structure for the repository-level results. I'll report the best DeepSeek CWM run and note the